---

## 3. Candidate Roles

| Role | General use | Security consideration |
|---|---|---|
| Reader | View resources | Low change capability |
| Contributor | Manage resources | Cannot manage RBAC access |
| Owner | Full resource management and access management | High privilege |

These are candidates, not automatic recommendations.

The actual role must be selected based on the task.

---

## 4. Scope Reduction

Consider:

```text
Subscription
    ↓
Resource Group
    ↓
Resource
```

If the task only requires access to one resource group, subscription-wide access should not be necessary.

If the task only requires one resource, resource-level scope may be preferable.

Document why the selected scope is sufficient.

---

## 5. Practical Exercise

1. Identify an existing or planned access scenario.
2. Determine what action the identity needs to perform.
3. Identify the current role and scope, if one exists.
4. Determine whether the permission is excessive.
5. Select a narrower role if appropriate.
6. Select a narrower scope if appropriate.
7. Apply the change.
8. Validate the required task.
9. Confirm that unnecessary privilege has been removed.
10. Capture evidence.

Do not claim success until the access has actually been tested.

---

## 6. PowerShell Investigation

Inspect the current role assignments:

```powershell
Get-AzRoleAssignment -SignInName "<USER-UPN>"
```

Inspect the proposed role:

```powershell
Get-AzRoleDefinition -Name "<ROLE>"
```

Inspect assignments at a specific scope:

```powershell
Get-AzRoleAssignment -Scope "<SCOPE>"
```

Record the actual output.

